  behaviour without naming an internal class. Verified at the base commit: the
  factory statement sits at line 135, the comment block opens at 136 and closes
  at 143, and the audit pattern matches exactly once in that file.

  THE STATIC AUDIT GATE IS TEXTUAL, NOT SEMANTIC. This is the crux and it is
  easy to get wrong. The gate is a pattern search over main source that must
  return zero hits, and at the base commit it returns exactly 7 hits across 5
  files. Only THREE of those seven are code the compiler sees. The other FOUR
  are one configuration string literal and THREE documentation comment blocks,
  and THIS MODULE OWNS ONE OF THOSE THREE COMMENT BLOCKS. Two consequences
  follow:

    * A build can compile perfectly, deploy perfectly and pass every functional
      smoke flow and STILL FAIL THE AUDIT GATE on this module's comment text.
      Anyone who reads the requirement as "remove the compile errors" will
      produce a change set that fails validation for reasons that look
      inexplicable in a build log.
    * A MODULE ACCESS JVM ARGUMENT COULD NEVER HAVE SATISFIED THIS GATE, however
      the rules were read, because the gate is textual and such an argument
      changes no text. The rule that forbids those arguments and the gate's
      textual nature INDEPENDENTLY FORECLOSE THE SAME SHORTCUT.

  THE EDIT TOUCHES NO EXECUTABLE STATEMENT, AND THAT WAS PROVEN RATHER THAN
  ASSUMED. Comparing the file at the base commit with the current revision
  yields 5 inserted and 6 deleted lines, every one of them inside the
  documentation comment block; a comparison filtered to exclude comment
  continuation and blank lines yields EXACTLY ZERO changed executable
